    {
      "id": 264454,
      "name": "",
      "username": "fjur",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/fjur/{size}/4849_2.png",
      "created_at": "2025-11-20T20:58:46.881Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"LonelyMan\" data-post=\"11\" data-topic=\"19877\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/l/f4b2a3/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> LonelyMan:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Do they seriously not have legal access to the term supervillain? That has to have been a other of their jokes, right?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Nope. DC and Marvel hold a joint trademark for both <em>superhero</em> and <em>supervillain</em>. You’ll note that none of the Sentinel Comics products include either word; SotM only has generic <em>heroes</em> and <em>villains</em>, and bills itself as a “Comic Book Card Game,” not a “Superhero Card Game.” Make of that what you will.</p>",
      "post_number": 12,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2025-11-20T20:58:46.881Z",
